A trivially copyable label for the interpolation union

`union Data` in `src/port/interpolation/FrameInterpolation.cpp` is the element type
of a `vector<Data>`, and a vector relocates its elements when it grows. libc++
static-asserts Cpp17MoveInsertable at that point, and the union fails it: one
variant member is a `label`, which was `std::pair<const void*, uintptr_t>`, and
libc++'s pair carries `__non_trivially_copyable_base`. A union with a variant
member whose copy constructor is non-trivial has its own copy constructor deleted,
so the vector cannot move the element anywhere.

libstdc++'s pair has no such base, which is why upstream builds elsewhere.

A plain aggregate instead. `label` is constructed as `{ a, b }` and compared as a
`map` key, so it needs the two members and `operator<` and nothing else; the
`.first`/`.second` spellings are not used on it anywhere in the file.

Ship of Harkinian carries the same patch against its own copy of this file - see
`../ship-of-harkinian/patches/0005`.

diff --git a/src/port/interpolation/FrameInterpolation.cpp b/src/port/interpolation/FrameInterpolation.cpp
index 1d30696..1d542bd 100644
--- a/src/port/interpolation/FrameInterpolation.cpp
+++ b/src/port/interpolation/FrameInterpolation.cpp
@@ -85,7 +85,17 @@ enum class Op {
     SetApplyMatrixTransformations,
 };
 
-typedef pair<const void*, uintptr_t> label;
+// A plain aggregate rather than a std::pair, because it is a member of the union below and that
+// union has to be trivially copyable for `vector<Data>` to relocate on growth. libc++'s pair
+// carries a non-trivially-copyable base, which deletes the union's copy constructor.
+struct label {
+    const void* ptr;
+    uintptr_t idx;
+
+    bool operator<(const label& o) const {
+        return ptr != o.ptr ? ptr < o.ptr : idx < o.idx;
+    }
+};
 
 union Data {
     Data() {
